# AI Backend Engineering Agent Context — Food Delivery Platform (.NET)

## 0. Project Goal

Build a production-style food ordering and delivery platform for learning and portfolio purposes.

The platform has:
- Customer Web application
- Customer Mobile application
- Driver/Delivery mobile application
- Admin/Operations Web application
- .NET backend
- Real-time order and driver-location tracking
- Maps and route visualization
- Restaurant/menu/order/payment/delivery workflows
- Authentication, authorization, notifications, background jobs, caching, observability, testing, Docker and deployment

The backend must be designed as a serious Junior-to-Mid-level portfolio project, not as a tutorial CRUD application.

Primary engineering goal:
- Apply the complete C#/.NET curriculum from Day 1 through Day 6.
- Use Clean Architecture + DDD where justified.
- Keep business rules inside the domain/application layers rather than controllers.
- Prefer maintainable, testable, observable code over premature complexity.

---

# 1. Day 1 — C# / .NET Fundamentals

## 1.1 Object-Oriented Programming

Know and apply:
- Class
- Object
- Fields
- Properties
- Methods
- Encapsulation
- Constructors
- Access modifiers
- `get`, `set`, `private set`, `init`
- Entity behavior
- Value types vs reference types
- `struct`
- Interface
- Abstract class
- Inheritance
- Polymorphism

Rules:
- Prefer encapsulation over public mutable state.
- Domain entities should protect their invariants through methods.
- Do not expose setters merely for EF convenience when a domain method is more appropriate.

Example:
```csharp
public sealed class Order
{
    public Guid Id { get; private set; }
    public OrderStatus Status { get; private set; }

    public void Confirm()
    {
        if (Status != OrderStatus.Pending)
            throw new DomainException("Only pending orders can be confirmed.");

        Status = OrderStatus.Confirmed;
    }
}
```

## 1.2 Generics

Understand:
- `T`
- Generic classes
- Generic methods
- Generic interfaces
- Generic constraints

Use generics for reusable infrastructure/application abstractions without sacrificing type safety.

## 1.3 Records

Know:
- `record`
- `record struct`
- Value-based equality
- Immutability patterns

Good candidates:
- DTOs
- Value Objects when appropriate
- Small immutable messages/commands

## 1.4 Nullable Reference Types

Use nullable reference types deliberately.

Rules:
- Avoid unnecessary `!` null-forgiving operators.
- Validate required input at boundaries.
- Represent optional data explicitly.
- Do not silently convert missing data into invalid defaults.

---

# 2. Day 2 — Collections, Delegates, LINQ and Query Thinking

## 2.1 Collections

Know when to use:
- `List<T>` — ordered collection with index access.
- `Dictionary<TKey,TValue>` — key/value lookup.
- `HashSet<T>` — uniqueness and membership checks.
- `IEnumerable<T>` — iteration abstraction.
- `IQueryable<T>` — provider-backed query composition.

## 2.2 LINQ

Use and understand:
- `Where`
- `Select`
- `SelectMany`
- `OrderBy`
- `ThenBy`
- `GroupBy`
- `Join`
- `Any`
- `All`
- `Contains`
- `First`
- `FirstOrDefault`
- `Single`
- `SingleOrDefault`
- `Count`
- `Sum`
- `Min`
- `Max`
- `Average`
- `ToList`
- `ToDictionary`

Understand deferred execution.

Avoid materializing data too early:
```csharp
var users = db.Users
    .Where(x => x.IsActive)
    .Select(x => new UserDto(...))
    .ToListAsync();
```

Do not do:
```csharp
var users = db.Users.ToList();
var active = users.Where(...);
```
when the database can perform the filtering.

## 2.3 Lambda Expressions

Understand lambdas as executable expressions used by:
- LINQ
- Delegates
- callbacks
- configuration
- predicates

## 2.4 Func / Action / Predicate

Know:
- `Func<T,...>` returns a value.
- `Action<T,...>` performs an action.
- `Predicate<T>` represents a boolean condition.

## 2.5 Expression Trees

Understand that `Expression<Func<T,bool>>` can represent a query expression that an ORM such as EF Core may translate to SQL.

---

# 3. Day 3 — Async, Exceptions, Resource Management and Background Work

## 3.1 Task / async / await

Understand asynchronous execution from first principles.

Use async APIs for I/O:
- Database
- HTTP
- Redis
- File storage
- External APIs

Prefer:
```csharp
public async Task<OrderDto> GetAsync(Guid id, CancellationToken ct)
{
    ...
}
```

Avoid blocking:
```csharp
.Result
.Wait()
```

Avoid unnecessary `Task.Run` for ordinary ASP.NET Core I/O.

## 3.2 Task.WhenAll

Use for independent asynchronous operations:
```csharp
var results = await Task.WhenAll(
    GetMenuAsync(ct),
    GetRestaurantAsync(ct),
    GetPromotionsAsync(ct));
```

Do not use parallel execution when operations depend on one another or share unsafe mutable state.

## 3.3 CancellationToken

Every meaningful application/infrastructure async operation should support cancellation where appropriate.

Pass the token through:
```csharp
await repository.GetAsync(id, cancellationToken);
```

ASP.NET Core request cancellation should propagate to database and external calls when safe.

## 3.4 Exceptions

Know:
- Built-in exceptions
- Custom exceptions
- Exception propagation
- Exception handling
- Global exception middleware

Do not use exceptions as ordinary business branching when a normal result is clearer.

Domain exceptions should represent violated business invariants.

## 3.5 IDisposable / using

Understand deterministic resource disposal.

Know:
- `IDisposable`
- `using`
- `using var`
- `IAsyncDisposable`
- `await using`

## 3.6 BackgroundService

Use `BackgroundService` for controlled background processing.

For serious workloads, prefer a durable queue/message broker architecture rather than an in-memory queue that loses jobs on restart.

Examples:
- Send notification
- Process order events
- Retry external integrations
- Cleanup expired records

---

# 4. Day 4 — Advanced C# and Engineering Fundamentals

## 4.1 Delegates

Understand delegate types and use cases:
- callbacks
- strategies
- events
- dependency injection abstractions

## 4.2 Events

Understand publisher/subscriber behavior.

Useful for in-process domain/application events, but do not confuse in-process events with durable distributed messaging.

## 4.3 Extension Methods

Use extension methods for readable reusable behavior without modifying the original type.

Avoid hiding important business logic inside excessive extension methods.

## 4.4 Dependency Injection

Use constructor injection by default.

Example:
```csharp
public sealed class OrderService(IOrderRepository repository)
{
}
```

Understand lifetimes:
- Transient
- Scoped
- Singleton

Important rule:
- `DbContext` is normally Scoped.
- Do not inject a Scoped dependency into a Singleton unless the lifetime boundary is intentionally handled.

## 4.5 SOLID

Apply:
- Single Responsibility Principle
- Open/Closed Principle
- Liskov Substitution Principle
- Interface Segregation Principle
- Dependency Inversion Principle

Do not apply SOLID mechanically. Prefer simple designs and introduce abstractions where they reduce coupling or protect business boundaries.

## 4.6 Reflection

Understand reflection for:
- runtime type inspection
- framework infrastructure
- metadata
- dependency registration scenarios

Do not use reflection for ordinary business logic when normal strongly typed code is clearer.

## 4.7 Attributes

Understand attributes and metadata.

Examples:
- Validation attributes
- ASP.NET Core attributes
- Serialization metadata
- Custom domain-independent metadata

## 4.8 IEnumerable / IEnumerator / yield

Understand iteration mechanics and lazy iteration.

Know the difference between:
- Returning a collection
- Returning `IEnumerable<T>`
- `yield return`
- Materialized results

## 4.9 Modern C#

Use modern C# features when they improve readability:
- Primary constructors
- Records
- Pattern matching
- Switch expressions
- Nullable reference types
- File-scoped namespaces
- Collection expressions where appropriate
- `required` members where useful
- Expression-bodied members when readable

Do not use modern syntax merely to make code shorter.

## 4.10 Concurrency / Thread Safety

Understand:
- Race conditions
- Shared mutable state
- Locks
- Thread-safe collections
- Atomic operations
- Immutability

ASP.NET Core services should generally avoid shared mutable state.

For counters, locks, caches and concurrent structures, choose a thread-safe design deliberately.

---

# 5. Day 5 — ASP.NET Core, Web API, EF Core and Production Backend Fundamentals

## 5.1 ASP.NET Core

Understand:
- HTTP request lifecycle
- Middleware pipeline
- Controllers
- Dependency injection
- Configuration
- Logging
- Authentication
- Authorization
- Filters
- Model binding
- Validation

Controller rule:
- Controllers should be thin.
- Controllers translate HTTP concerns into application commands/queries.
- Business logic belongs in application/domain layers.

## 5.2 REST / HTTP

Know:
- GET
- POST
- PUT
- PATCH
- DELETE
- Status codes
- Headers
- Request body
- Query string
- Route parameters

Important status codes:
- `200 OK`
- `201 Created`
- `204 No Content`
- `400 Bad Request`
- `401 Unauthorized`
- `403 Forbidden`
- `404 Not Found`
- `409 Conflict`
- `422 Unprocessable Content`
- `500 Internal Server Error`

Use HTTP semantics consistently.

## 5.3 EF Core

Understand:
- ORM
- Entity
- `DbContext`
- `DbSet`
- CRUD
- LINQ translation
- Change tracking
- `AsNoTracking`
- Migrations
- Relationships
- Foreign keys
- Navigation properties
- `Include`
- Fluent API

Remember:
- EF Core is an ORM, not the database itself.
- `DbContext` already has Unit-of-Work semantics.
- A custom Unit-of-Work abstraction is optional and must be justified.

## 5.4 IQueryable vs IEnumerable

`IQueryable<T>` should normally remain queryable while composing a database query.

`IEnumerable<T>` operates on materialized/in-memory data.

Avoid accidental early materialization.

## 5.5 Tracking / No Tracking

Use tracking when modifying entities.

Use `AsNoTracking()` for read-only queries when change tracking is unnecessary.

## 5.6 Relationships

Understand:
- One-to-one
- One-to-many
- Many-to-many
- FK
- Navigation properties
- Cascade behavior

## 5.7 N+1

Detect and prevent N+1 query patterns.

Use:
- Projection
- Proper joins
- `Include` where appropriate
- Explicit query design

Prefer DTO projection for read APIs when it avoids loading unnecessary entities.

## 5.8 Dependency Injection and Lifetimes

Default to constructor injection.

Use:
- Scoped for request/unit-of-work-oriented services
- Singleton for stateless thread-safe application-wide infrastructure
- Transient for lightweight stateless services when appropriate

## 5.9 Middleware

Know how to build:
- Exception middleware
- Correlation/request ID middleware
- Logging middleware
- Authentication pipeline

Middleware should handle cross-cutting HTTP concerns, not domain business rules.

## 5.10 Authentication

Understand:
- Authentication = who are you?
- Authorization = what are you allowed to do?

Use JWT access tokens for suitable API authentication scenarios.

Understand:
- Claims
- Token expiration
- Refresh tokens
- Roles
- Policies
- `[Authorize]`
- `[AllowAnonymous]`

## 5.11 Password Security

Never store plaintext passwords.

Use a proven password hashing mechanism/library.

Do not implement cryptographic password hashing manually.

## 5.12 RBAC and Policy Authorization

Use roles for broad access control.

Use policies/permissions for fine-grained rules.

Examples:
- Customer
- Driver
- RestaurantStaff
- RestaurantManager
- Admin
- Operations

## 5.13 DTOs

Do not bind request DTOs directly to persistence entities.

Use separate models for:
- Request DTO
- Response DTO
- Domain Entity
- Persistence model where justified

Reason:
- Prevent over-posting
- Protect invariants
- Decouple API contracts from database structure

## 5.14 Validation

Use request validation for input shape and basic constraints.

Examples:
- Required
- Length
- Email format
- Numeric ranges

Business validation belongs in application/domain logic.

Example:
- `Quantity > 0` can be request validation.
- `Restaurant must be open to accept an order` is a business rule.

## 5.15 Exception Handling

Use centralized exception handling.

Map known exceptions to consistent HTTP responses.

Never leak stack traces or internal infrastructure details to clients in production.

## 5.16 Logging

Use `ILogger<T>` and structured logging.

Include useful context:
- Request ID / Correlation ID
- User ID where appropriate
- Order ID
- Restaurant ID
- Driver ID
- Operation name

Never log:
- Passwords
- Access tokens
- Refresh tokens
- Sensitive personal data unnecessarily

## 5.17 Configuration

Use:
- `appsettings.json`
- Environment variables
- Options pattern

Secrets must not be committed to source control.

Examples:
- Database connection string
- JWT configuration
- Redis connection
- Map provider key
- Payment provider key
- Object storage credentials

## 5.18 Testing

Use:
- Unit tests
- Integration tests
- End-to-end tests where valuable

Understand:
- xUnit
- AAA pattern
- Test doubles/mocking
- `WebApplicationFactory`
- Test database
- Testcontainers concept
- Code coverage
- CI testing

Testing goal:
- Protect business behavior and critical workflows.
- 100% coverage is not the goal by itself.

---

# 6. Day 6 — Clean Architecture + DDD

## 6.1 Architecture Layers

Preferred structure:

```text
src/
  FoodDelivery.Domain/
  FoodDelivery.Application/
  FoodDelivery.Infrastructure/
  FoodDelivery.API/

tests/
  FoodDelivery.Domain.Tests/
  FoodDelivery.Application.Tests/
  FoodDelivery.IntegrationTests/
```

Responsibilities:

### Domain
- Entities
- Value Objects
- Aggregates
- Domain Services
- Domain Events
- Business rules
- Domain exceptions

Must remain independent of infrastructure/framework concerns as much as practical.

### Application
- Commands
- Queries
- Use cases
- DTOs
- Application services
- Repository/port abstractions
- External service abstractions
- Transaction orchestration

### Infrastructure
- EF Core
- PostgreSQL
- Redis
- Message broker
- Email/SMS/push integrations
- Maps provider integration
- Payment integration
- File/object storage
- Repository implementations

### API / Presentation
- Controllers/endpoints
- Authentication configuration
- Middleware
- HTTP concerns
- API documentation
- Composition root / dependency registration

Dependency direction:

```text
API ───────────────┐
Infrastructure ────┼──> Application ───> Domain
                   │
                   └──> implements Application abstractions
```

The exact placement of repository interfaces can vary. Prefer Application ports for use-case-oriented persistence abstractions; Domain interfaces are appropriate when the abstraction is genuinely part of the domain model.

## 6.2 Entity

Entity has identity and lifecycle.

Example:
- Order
- Restaurant
- Customer
- Driver
- Delivery

Entity invariants should be protected internally.

## 6.3 Value Object

Value Object is identified by value rather than identity.

Examples:
- Money
- Address
- GeoCoordinate
- PhoneNumber
- Email
- OrderNumber

Example:
```csharp
public sealed record GeoCoordinate(decimal Latitude, decimal Longitude);
```

Validate invariants at construction/factory boundaries.

## 6.4 Aggregate / Aggregate Root

An Aggregate is a consistency boundary.

The Aggregate Root is the only public entry point for modifying the aggregate's internal state.

Food-delivery examples:
- `Order` aggregate containing order items.
- `Restaurant` aggregate containing menu concepts where consistency requires it.

Do not make every database table an aggregate.

Keep aggregate boundaries small enough to avoid unnecessary transaction contention.

## 6.5 Domain Service

Use a Domain Service when a business rule:
- Is domain-specific
- Does not naturally belong to one entity/value object
- Requires multiple domain concepts

Do not create a Domain Service for every CRUD operation.

## 6.6 Repository Pattern

Repository abstracts persistence from business/application logic.

Example:
```csharp
public interface IOrderRepository
{
    Task<Order?> GetByIdAsync(Guid id, CancellationToken ct);
    Task AddAsync(Order order, CancellationToken ct);
}
```

Infrastructure implements it with EF Core.

Avoid generic repositories that merely duplicate every `DbSet` method without adding value.

## 6.7 Unit of Work

Understand Unit of Work as a transaction/change persistence boundary.

EF Core `DbContext` already provides this behavior.

Do not add `IUnitOfWork` solely because a tutorial says every project must have one.

Add an abstraction only when it improves application-level orchestration or testing/design clarity.

## 6.8 CQRS

CQRS = Command Query Responsibility Segregation.

Command:
- Changes state.
- Example: `PlaceOrderCommand`.

Query:
- Reads state.
- Example: `GetOrderTrackingQuery`.

CQRS does NOT automatically mean:
- Microservices
- Separate databases
- Event sourcing

Use CQRS when it makes the application model clearer.

## 6.9 DTO Mapping

Map explicitly between:
- Request DTO
- Command
- Domain model
- Entity
- Response DTO

Do not expose domain entities directly from public APIs.

## 6.10 Business-first Thinking

Analyze requirements in this order:
1. Actors
2. Use cases
3. Business rules
4. Entities
5. Value Objects
6. Aggregates
7. Commands
8. Queries
9. Persistence needs
10. External integrations
11. API contracts

Do not start by creating database tables blindly.

---

# 7. Food Delivery Domain Model

Core bounded areas/modules:

- Identity & Access
- Customer
- Restaurant
- Menu
- Cart
- Order
- Payment
- Delivery
- Driver
- Location Tracking
- Promotion
- Notification
- Rating/Review
- Admin/Operations

Suggested core entities:
- User
- CustomerProfile
- DriverProfile
- Restaurant
- RestaurantBranch
- Menu
- MenuItem
- Cart
- CartItem
- Order
- OrderItem
- Payment
- Delivery
- DeliveryAssignment
- DriverLocation
- Promotion
- Review
- Notification

Potential value objects:
- Money
- Address
- GeoCoordinate
- PhoneNumber
- Email
- OrderNumber
- DeliveryFee

Important aggregate examples:
- Order is responsible for its lifecycle and order items.
- Delivery controls delivery assignment/status rules.
- Restaurant controls business rules related to accepting orders and availability where appropriate.

Order lifecycle example:
```text
Pending
  -> Confirmed
  -> Preparing
  -> ReadyForPickup
  -> PickedUp
  -> Delivering
  -> Delivered
```

Alternative failure/cancellation states:
```text
Pending -> Cancelled
Confirmed -> Cancelled
```

Never allow arbitrary status mutation from controllers.

---

# 8. API / Communication Strategy

Do NOT force one protocol to handle every workload.

Use a hybrid API architecture.

## 8.1 REST — Primary external business API

Use REST for:
- Authentication
- Restaurants
- Menus
- Cart
- Orders
- Payments initiation/status
- Customer profile
- Admin CRUD/operations

Why:
- Excellent browser/mobile compatibility
- Simple HTTP semantics
- Easy debugging
- Strong ASP.NET Core support
- Easy OpenAPI/Swagger documentation
- Suitable for most business CRUD/use-case APIs

REST should remain the main API, not be eliminated just because other protocols exist.

## 8.2 WebSocket — Real-time tracking

Use WebSocket for:
- Driver location updates to customers
- Order status updates
- Driver assignment changes
- Restaurant order board updates
- Operations dashboard live updates

Reason:
- Bidirectional persistent connection
- Low latency
- Server can push events immediately

Do not send every GPS point to every client blindly. Use throttling, authorization and appropriate location frequency.

## 8.3 gRPC — Internal service-to-service communication

Use gRPC if/when the backend is split into independently deployable services.

Good candidates:
- Order service -> Pricing service
- Delivery service -> Routing service
- Notification service -> user/device service

Why:
- Strong contracts via protobuf
- Efficient binary protocol
- Streaming support
- Good internal-service performance

Do not introduce microservices only to use gRPC. A modular monolith can use REST + WebSocket first.

## 8.4 GraphQL — Complex read aggregation

Potentially use GraphQL for:
- Mobile home screen aggregation
- Admin dashboards with many related read models
- Flexible restaurant/menu discovery

Why:
- Client selects exactly the fields it needs.
- Reduces multiple read requests for highly variable screens.

But GraphQL is NOT required for the first version. REST + optimized query endpoints may be simpler.

## 8.5 SSE — One-way live feeds

Use SSE when the client mainly needs server-to-client updates and does not need bidirectional messaging.

Good candidates:
- Admin live order feed
- Operations monitoring
- Long-running job progress

For customer/driver interactive tracking, WebSocket is usually the stronger choice.

## 8.6 Webhooks — External provider events

Use webhooks for provider-to-platform callbacks:
- Payment provider confirms payment
- Map/third-party provider event where supported
- External delivery/payment status callbacks

Webhook endpoint requirements:
- Signature verification
- Idempotency
- Replay protection where supported
- Fast acknowledgement
- Asynchronous processing
- Durable event recording

## 8.7 MQTT — High-frequency device/location telemetry

Potentially use MQTT for driver-device location telemetry if the platform grows to high-frequency location traffic.

Flow:
```text
Driver App
   -> MQTT Broker
      -> Location Consumer
         -> Redis / Stream / Database
            -> Tracking Gateway
               -> WebSocket
                  -> Customer App
```

Why:
- Lightweight
- Designed for device telemetry
- Publish/subscribe model
- Efficient for many connected clients

However, do NOT add MQTT in V1 if the traffic is small. REST/WebSocket can be enough initially. Introduce MQTT when the system actually needs high-frequency telemetry at scale.

---

# 9. Recommended V1 Architecture

Start as a Modular Monolith, not microservices.

```text
                    ┌─────────────────────┐
                    │ Next.js Web         │
                    │ Customer/Admin      │
                    └──────────┬──────────┘
                               │ REST / WS
                    ┌──────────▼──────────┐
                    │ React Native Apps   │
                    │ Customer + Driver   │
                    └──────────┬──────────┘
                               │
                        ┌──────▼──────┐
                        │ API Gateway │
                        │ / ASP.NET   │
                        └──────┬──────┘
                               │
             ┌─────────────────┼─────────────────┐
             │                 │                 │
       Application         WebSocket          Webhooks
       / Domain            Hub                Payments
             │
       Infrastructure
             │
    ┌────────┼────────┬────────────┐
    │        │        │            │
PostgreSQL Redis   Message      Object
                    Broker       Storage
```

Recommended initial deployment:
- One ASP.NET Core backend
- PostgreSQL
- Redis
- Background worker
- WebSocket/SignalR
- Object storage
- External map provider
- External payment provider
- Push notification provider

Split into microservices only after module boundaries and scaling needs are understood.

---

# 10. Backend Technology Recommendation

Primary backend:
- C#
- .NET / ASP.NET Core
- EF Core
- PostgreSQL
- Redis
- SignalR for WebSocket-style real-time communication
- BackgroundService initially; durable queue/broker when needed
- OpenAPI/Swagger
- xUnit
- Integration tests
- Docker

Optional later:
- RabbitMQ / Kafka depending on actual event requirements
- MQTT broker for high-volume driver telemetry
- gRPC for internal service communication
- OpenTelemetry for distributed tracing/metrics

Use SignalR rather than manually implementing raw WebSocket protocol unless raw WebSocket behavior is specifically needed. SignalR provides connection management, groups and client abstractions while still supporting WebSocket transport.

---

# 11. Frontend Recommendation

## Web

Recommended:
- Next.js
- TypeScript
- React
- TanStack Query
- Tailwind CSS or a consistent component system
- MapLibre GL JS or a suitable map SDK
- WebSocket/SignalR client

Why Next.js:
- React ecosystem
- Routing
- Server/client rendering options
- Good production structure
- TypeScript support
- Easy portfolio deployment

For an authenticated dashboard/application, do not overuse SSR. Use client-side data fetching where appropriate.

## Mobile

Recommended:
- React Native + Expo for faster development, unless native/background-location requirements justify bare React Native/native modules.
- TypeScript
- TanStack Query
- React Navigation
- SignalR client
- Native location APIs
- Map SDK

The mobile platform should be split logically into:
- Customer app
- Driver app

They may share a monorepo and common packages but must have separate permission/security concerns.

Driver app requirements are stricter because background location, battery use, GPS accuracy and connectivity are involved.

---

# 12. Real-Time Order Tracking Design

Desired behavior:

```text
Driver GPS
   ↓
Driver Mobile App
   ↓
Location ingestion
   ↓
Redis / short-lived location state
   ↓
Delivery Tracking Service
   ↓
SignalR Hub
   ↓
Customer App/Web
   ↓
Live map marker
```

Order status:
```text
Restaurant confirms
      ↓
Order domain event
      ↓
Application handler
      ↓
Notification + SignalR event
      ↓
Customer sees status immediately
```

Location data should be treated differently from business state:
- Current location can live in Redis for fast reads.
- Durable location history should be stored only at a justified sampling frequency.
- Do not write every GPS coordinate directly to PostgreSQL at high frequency.

Security:
- Customer may only subscribe to their own order tracking channel.
- Driver may publish location only for an active assigned delivery.
- Admin/operations may have broader authorized visibility.

---

# 13. Map Integration

Map responsibilities:
- Display restaurant locations
- Display customer delivery address
- Display driver current position
- Calculate/display route
- Estimate distance/time
- Optionally geocode addresses

Do not put map-provider logic directly in domain code.

Use an abstraction such as:
```csharp
public interface IMapService
{
    Task<RouteResult> CalculateRouteAsync(
        GeoCoordinate origin,
        GeoCoordinate destination,
        CancellationToken ct);
}
```

Infrastructure implements the chosen provider.

Never expose provider API secrets to the client unless the provider explicitly requires public client-side keys with restrictions.

---

# 14. Payment Design

Payment must be treated as an external integration.

Flow:
```text
Customer
  -> Create Order
  -> Payment Pending
  -> Create Payment Intent
  -> Provider
  -> Customer completes payment
  -> Provider webhook
  -> Verify signature
  -> Idempotent payment processing
  -> Mark payment successful
  -> Confirm order/payment state
```

Never trust a client-side "payment successful" flag.

Use idempotency keys for payment/order operations where appropriate.

---

# 15. Redis Usage

Good uses:
- Current driver location
- Short-lived order tracking state
- Caching restaurant/menu reads
- Rate limiting
- OTP/temporary verification data where appropriate
- Distributed locks only when truly necessary
- Session/ephemeral state where justified

Do not use Redis as the authoritative permanent database for orders/payments.

---

# 16. Background Processing

Use background processing for:
- Notifications
- Emails/SMS
- Payment reconciliation
- Expired carts
- Order timeout handling
- Promotion processing
- Location cleanup
- Retryable external operations

Prefer durable queues for jobs that must survive application restarts.

Every job should consider:
- Idempotency
- Retry policy
- Dead-letter handling
- Logging
- Correlation IDs

---

# 17. Security Rules

Mandatory:
- JWT authentication
- Refresh-token strategy where appropriate
- Role/policy authorization
- Password hashing via trusted implementation
- Input validation
- Rate limiting on sensitive endpoints
- CORS configuration
- HTTPS
- Secret management through environment/secret store
- Webhook signature validation
- Idempotency for critical operations
- Avoid over-posting
- Avoid leaking internal errors

Never:
- Store plaintext passwords
- Store secrets in Git
- Trust client role claims without validated authentication
- Trust client payment success state
- Allow arbitrary order status mutation
- Allow a driver to update another driver's delivery

---

# 18. Observability

Use structured logs and correlation IDs.

Recommended later:
- OpenTelemetry traces
- Metrics
- Error tracking
- Request latency
- Database query timing
- Queue depth
- WebSocket connection counts
- Location ingestion rate
- Order creation success/failure rate
- Payment failure rate

Important business metrics:
- Orders per minute
- Average preparation time
- Average delivery time
- Cancellation rate
- Payment failure rate
- Driver assignment latency

---

# 19. Testing Strategy

Unit tests:
- Domain rules
- Order transitions
- Pricing
- Promotion rules
- Delivery assignment rules

Integration tests:
- EF Core + PostgreSQL
- Authentication
- Order API
- Payment webhook
- Redis integration

End-to-end tests:
- Register/login
- Browse restaurant
- Add to cart
- Place order
- Pay
- Restaurant accepts
- Driver assigned
- Driver updates location
- Customer sees tracking
- Delivery completed

Critical business flows should have strong automated coverage.

---

# 20. API Design Principles

Prefer use-case-oriented endpoints where appropriate instead of exposing database CRUD blindly.

Examples:
```text
POST   /api/v1/auth/login
POST   /api/v1/auth/refresh
GET    /api/v1/restaurants
GET    /api/v1/restaurants/{id}
GET    /api/v1/restaurants/{id}/menu
POST   /api/v1/cart/items
PATCH  /api/v1/cart/items/{id}
POST   /api/v1/orders
GET    /api/v1/orders/{id}
POST   /api/v1/orders/{id}/cancel
POST   /api/v1/orders/{id}/payment
GET    /api/v1/orders/{id}/tracking
POST   /api/v1/deliveries/{id}/accept
POST   /api/v1/deliveries/{id}/location
POST   /api/v1/payments/webhook
```

Do not expose:
```text
PUT /orders/{id}
```
for arbitrary state changes if that allows clients to set internal fields such as status, payment state or assigned driver.

Prefer explicit commands:
```text
POST /orders/{id}/cancel
POST /orders/{id}/confirm
POST /orders/{id}/assign-driver
POST /deliveries/{id}/pickup
POST /deliveries/{id}/complete
```

---

# 21. Idempotency

Critical operations should be safe against retries.

Examples:
- Create order
- Payment initiation
- Payment webhook
- Driver acceptance
- Delivery completion

Use idempotency keys or unique business identifiers where appropriate.

Example:
```text
Idempotency-Key: 7c0d...
```

A repeated request with the same key should not create duplicate orders/payments.

---

# 22. Pagination / Filtering / Sorting

Collection APIs should support controlled pagination.

Prefer cursor pagination for large/high-frequency datasets where appropriate.

Examples:
```text
GET /restaurants?cursor=...&limit=20
GET /orders?status=delivering&limit=20
```

Do not accept unlimited `pageSize` from clients.

---

# 23. Coding Standards for the AI

When generating backend code:

1. Use C# with nullable reference types enabled.
2. Prefer async APIs and `CancellationToken` for I/O.
3. Use constructor injection.
4. Keep controllers/endpoints thin.
5. Keep domain rules in Domain.
6. Keep orchestration/use cases in Application.
7. Keep EF Core/external providers in Infrastructure.
8. Do not expose EF entities directly from API responses.
9. Prefer explicit DTOs.
10. Validate at boundaries and enforce invariants in the domain.
11. Use structured logging.
12. Do not swallow exceptions.
13. Do not use `.Result` or `.Wait()` in ASP.NET Core request code.
14. Avoid unnecessary abstractions.
15. Avoid generic repository boilerplate without value.
16. Avoid unnecessary Unit-of-Work wrappers around `DbContext`.
17. Avoid premature microservices.
18. Avoid premature CQRS complexity.
19. Use database transactions when multiple state changes must be atomic.
20. Design critical commands to be idempotent.
21. Use projections for read-heavy endpoints when appropriate.
22. Watch for N+1 queries.
23. Never trust client-provided authorization/payment state.
24. Never hard-code secrets.
25. Write tests for important business behavior.

---

