---

# 25. Definition of Done for the Portfolio Project

The project is considered strong when the developer can explain:

- Why Clean Architecture is used.
- Why Domain rules are not placed in controllers.
- Why Order is an Aggregate Root.
- Which concepts are Entities vs Value Objects.
- Why EF Core belongs in Infrastructure.
- Why DTOs are separated from entities.
- How JWT authentication works.
- Authentication vs authorization.
- Scoped vs Singleton vs Transient.
- How async/await works.
- Why CancellationToken is used.
- How EF Core translates LINQ to SQL.
- IQueryable vs IEnumerable.
- How to prevent N+1 queries.
- Why Redis is used.
- Why WebSocket/SignalR is used for tracking.
- When gRPC is useful.
- When GraphQL is useful.
- Why webhooks must be idempotent and verified.
- When MQTT is justified.
- Why REST remains useful.
- How payment security works.
- How driver location reaches the customer.
- How background jobs work.
- How testing protects business rules.
- How Docker/deployment works.
- How to debug production failures using logs/traces/metrics.

The developer should be able to implement a feature end-to-end:

```text
Requirement
  -> Domain rule
  -> Entity / Value Object
  -> Aggregate behavior
  -> Command / Query
  -> Application service/handler
  -> Repository/port
  -> Infrastructure
  -> API endpoint
  -> DTO
  -> Tests
  -> Logging/observability
  -> Frontend integration
```

---

# 26. AI Agent Behavior

When asked to implement a feature:

1. First identify the business requirement.
2. Identify affected bounded area/module.
3. Identify domain rules/invariants.
4. Decide whether a new Entity, Value Object, Aggregate behavior or Domain Service is needed.
5. Define Application command/query/use case.
6. Define interfaces/ports required by Application.
7. Implement Infrastructure adapters.
8. Add API endpoint/contract.
9. Add validation and authorization.
10. Add unit/integration tests.
11. Add logging and error handling.
12. Consider idempotency/concurrency.
13. Explain database/index implications.
14. Only then suggest frontend integration.

When asked to modify code, preserve existing architecture unless there is a strong reason to refactor it.

Do not create layers/files just for ceremony.

Do not introduce a technology because it is fashionable. Every infrastructure component must have a concrete reason.

For this project, prefer a Modular Monolith first and evolve toward distributed services only when scale or ownership boundaries justify it.

# End of Agent Context

# 27. Universal Documentation Engineering Standard

These documentation rules are UNIVERSAL and MUST be applied to every project, regardless of language, framework, architecture, database, or domain.

## 27.1 Documentation is part of implementation

A feature is NOT considered complete until its implementation, tests, and documentation are aligned.

For every meaningful feature/change, the agent MUST evaluate whether these documents need to be created or updated:

- `docs/README.md` — system-level overview when architecture or major capabilities change.
- `docs/features/<feature>.md` — feature behavior, business rules, use cases, permissions, APIs, data, tests, and edge cases.
- `docs/architecture/<topic>.md` — architecture decisions or structural changes.
- `docs/api/<topic>.md` — public/internal API contract changes when applicable.
- `docs/flows/<flow>.md` — important end-to-end workflows.
- `docs/security/<topic>.md` — security/authentication/authorization/data-protection changes.
- `docs/decisions/ADR-xxx.md` — significant architectural or technology decisions.

Do not postpone documentation as a final cleanup step.

## 27.2 Documentation must describe reality

Documentation MUST describe the implemented system, not the desired system.

Never document planned behavior as implemented behavior.

When implementation differs from existing documentation, update the documentation in the same change whenever practical.

If a feature is partially implemented, explicitly mark its status, scope, and limitations.

## 27.3 Required system documentation structure

When a project uses a `docs/` directory, prefer this structure unless the existing repository has a justified alternative:

```text
docs/
├── README.md
├── features/
│   ├── README.md
│   └── <feature>.md
├── architecture/
│   └── <topic>.md
├── api/
│   └── <topic>.md
├── flows/
│   └── <flow>.md
├── security/
│   └── <topic>.md
└── decisions/
    └── ADR-xxx-<decision>.md
```

The exact folder structure may be adapted to an existing project, but documentation MUST remain discoverable and logically grouped.

## 27.4 System README requirements

The main `docs/README.md` SHOULD provide enough context for a new engineer to understand the project without reading source code first.

It SHOULD cover:

1. Project purpose
2. Business/domain overview
3. System actors
4. Major user journeys
5. Architecture overview
6. Technology stack
7. Repository/module structure
8. Domain model overview
9. Data/storage overview
10. Authentication
11. Authorization and permission model
12. API/communication strategy
13. Background processing
14. Caching
15. Realtime behavior when applicable
16. External integrations
17. Testing strategy
18. Security baseline
19. Observability
20. Local development
21. Environment/configuration
22. Deployment
23. Known limitations
24. Future/possible evolution

Prefer diagrams where they communicate relationships or flows more clearly than prose.

## 27.5 Feature documentation standard

Each significant feature document SHOULD contain:

```text
# Feature

## 1. Overview
## 2. Actors
## 3. Business Requirements
## 4. Business Rules / Invariants
## 5. State / Lifecycle
## 6. Use Cases
## 7. Domain Model
## 8. Authorization
## 9. API / Events
## 10. Data Model
## 11. Side Effects
## 12. Background Jobs
## 13. Realtime Behavior
## 14. Error Cases
## 15. Security Considerations
## 16. Tests
## 17. Edge Cases
## 18. Known Limitations
```

Not every section is mandatory for every feature; omit sections that genuinely do not apply rather than filling them with meaningless text.

## 27.6 API documentation standard

Every documented endpoint SHOULD state:

- HTTP method
- path
- purpose
- authentication requirement
- permission requirement
- resource/tenant scope
- request body/query/path parameters
- validation rules
- response shape
- relevant status codes
- error cases
- side effects
- idempotency behavior where applicable
- pagination/filter/sort behavior where applicable

Do not document database entities as if they were public API contracts.

## 27.7 Architecture documentation standard

Document architecture at the level necessary to explain boundaries and responsibilities.

Useful topics include:

- system architecture
- backend architecture
- frontend architecture
- mobile architecture
- domain/module boundaries
- database design
- authorization model
- realtime architecture
- deployment architecture
- external integrations

Architecture documentation MUST explain important reasons, not merely list technologies.

Bad:

```text
We use Redis, SignalR, PostgreSQL, Docker and CQRS.
```

Good:

```text
PostgreSQL is the source of truth for transactional order state.
Redis stores short-lived/high-read data such as current driver location.
SignalR delivers realtime state changes to connected clients.
CQRS is used only for read/write paths where the separation materially improves the design.
```

## 27.8 Architecture Decision Records (ADR)

Create an ADR when a decision has meaningful long-term consequences, trade-offs, or migration cost.

Use this structure:

```md
# ADR-XXX: Decision Title

## Status
Accepted | Proposed | Superseded | Deprecated

## Context

## Decision

## Alternatives Considered

## Consequences

## Security / Operational Impact

## Migration / Rollback
```

Do not create ADRs for trivial implementation details.

## 27.9 Flow documentation

Important workflows SHOULD be documented from actor to final outcome.

Example:

```text
Actor
  -> API/UI action
  -> Authentication
  -> Authorization
  -> Application use case
  -> Domain rules
  -> Persistence/external system
  -> Event/job/realtime side effect
  -> Final client state
```

State transitions MUST be explicit for lifecycle-heavy features such as:
- orders
- payments
- deliveries
- approval workflows
- tickets
- subscriptions
- jobs
- onboarding

Document valid transitions and invalid transitions.

## 27.10 Security documentation

Document security-sensitive behavior when applicable:

- authentication
- authorization
- roles and permissions
- resource/tenant scope
- secrets/configuration
- file uploads
- payment verification
- webhook signature verification
- token lifecycle
- rate limiting
- audit logging
- sensitive data handling
- retention/deletion policy

Never place real secrets, production tokens, private keys, or credentials in documentation.

## 27.11 Permission documentation

When a system has roles and permissions, document all three dimensions when applicable:

```text
Role
  -> Permission
  -> Resource / Tenant / Branch Scope
```

Document UI/screen permissions separately from server-side authorization behavior when both exist.

Make it explicit that frontend permission checks are UX controls and server-side checks are the security boundary.

## 27.12 Database documentation

Document important data relationships, ownership, constraints, indexes, and consistency boundaries.

The documentation SHOULD distinguish:

- Entity/table
- Aggregate
- Aggregate Root
- Value Object
- External resource

Do not assume one table equals one domain aggregate.

## 27.13 Realtime / asynchronous documentation

When using queues, workers, events, WebSockets, SignalR, SSE, MQTT, or similar mechanisms, document:

- producer
- consumer
- message/event meaning
- delivery semantics
- ordering assumptions
- retries
- idempotency
- failure handling
- reconnect behavior
- source of truth

Realtime transport MUST NOT be described as the authoritative persistence layer unless the system is explicitly designed that way.

## 27.14 Documentation and source-code truth

When possible, documentation should reference concrete artifacts rather than vague descriptions:

- actual API paths
- actual module names
- actual configuration keys (without secret values)
- actual database concepts
- actual message/event names
- actual state names
- actual permission identifiers

Avoid documenting hypothetical classes or future interfaces as current behavior.

## 27.15 AI agent documentation workflow

For every feature request, the agent MUST follow:

```text
Understand requirement
  -> Inspect existing docs
  -> Inspect source/code structure
  -> Implement
  -> Test
  -> Verify behavior
  -> Update affected docs
  -> Inspect diff
```

Before changing architecture, read the relevant architecture docs and ADRs first.

Before changing a feature, read its feature document first when it exists.

If source code and docs conflict, verify actual behavior and then reconcile the documentation.

## 27.16 Documentation quality rules

Documentation MUST be:
- accurate
- concise enough to remain maintainable
- structured
- searchable
- internally consistent
- explicit about assumptions
- explicit about limitations

Avoid:
- marketing language in engineering docs
- unexplained acronyms
- giant unstructured paragraphs
- duplicated conflicting explanations
- stale code examples
- fake completeness

Prefer diagrams, tables, state diagrams, and examples when they reduce ambiguity.

## 27.17 Documentation versioning

When behavior changes:

1. Update affected feature docs.
2. Update API docs if contract changes.
3. Update architecture docs if boundaries change.
4. Add/update an ADR when a significant decision changes.
5. Update flow/security docs when behavior affects them.

Do not leave known stale documentation intentionally unless it is explicitly marked as deprecated/historical.

## 27.18 Definition of Done — Documentation

A feature/change is documentation-complete when:

- The relevant feature document exists or is updated.
- Business rules are recorded.
- Authorization is recorded.
- Important API contracts are recorded.
- Important state transitions are recorded.
- Relevant security implications are recorded.
- Important architecture decisions are recorded.
- Known edge cases/limitations are recorded.
- System-level documentation is updated when necessary.
- No documentation claims behavior that the current code does not implement.

# 28. Universal Project Deliverables

For a mature project, the repository SHOULD contain:

```text
.agent
README.md

docs/
├── README.md
├── features/
├── architecture/
├── api/
├── flows/
├── security/
└── decisions/

tests/
src/ or app/
```

The exact source/test structure depends on the technology stack.

`.agent` defines how the AI should engineer the project.
`docs/` defines what the project actually does and why.
Source code defines the implemented behavior.
Tests provide executable evidence for important behavior.

These four should remain consistent:

```text
.agent
   ↓ engineering rules
Source Code
   ↓ implementation
Tests
   ↓ executable verification
Docs
   ↓ human/system knowledge
```

# 29. Final Universal Rule

Always prefer:

```text
Correctness
> Security
> Maintainability
> Testability
> Observability
> Simplicity
> Performance optimization
> Architectural sophistication
```

Architecture, patterns, frameworks, protocols, and tools are means—not goals.

For every project, use the smallest set of concepts and technologies that can satisfy the real requirements while leaving a clear path for future evolution.
